A finite strict-interior dual has $H=\begin{pmatrix}a_H&c_H\\c_H&b_H\end{pmatrix}$ and $\eta>0$. Its whole-circle inequalities are precisely

$$
\begin{aligned}
\psi_0(t)&=\eta J(t)+a_H+(b_H-a_H)t-2|c_H|v(t)\ge0,\\
\psi_1(t)&=\eta J(t)+a_H+d_0+(b_H-a_H-d_0)t-2|c_H+\zeta|v(t)\ge0
\end{aligned}
\tag{TB.5}
$$

for every $t\in[0,1]$. Both gaps are strictly convex. Finite dual attainment follows by mixing a small uniform-circle covariance measure into the critical measure while retaining strict budget slack. Its positive density permits independent small $\cos(2\vartheta)$ and $\sin(2\vartheta)$ covariance perturbations. Thus the covariance/budget projection has an interior neighborhood. Separation of the compact moment hull with budget/objective slack has nonzero objective coefficient and yields a finite normalized dual. An $\eta=0$ dual would have $H\ge0,H\ge\Xi$ and $\operatorname{tr}H\ge\gamma$, contradicting strict unsaturation. Active cost follows from JS strict increase.

Complementarity and strict convexity leave one positive contact, at $p>p_z$ with negative cross sign, and negative contacts at one $q\in(0,1/2)$. One has $c_H\le0<c_H+\zeta$. A zero-expectation contact would zero both gaps at the positive contact coordinate, where neither sign has zero expectation; it is excluded. Axes cannot cancel the positive contact's nonzero cross moment. If $c_H<0$, the two lines must be orthogonal with unit masses. If $c_H=0$, there can be two negative lines. Their at-most-three projectors are independent: three distinct Bloch-circle points cannot be collinear. Covariance fixes the weights for a fixed dual. This proves equality uniqueness; an affine interval of $\Phi$ would force this same measure to have two different active costs, proving strict concavity.

The explicit alternatives and certificates are as follows. A projective pair consists of

$$
e_P=(\sqrt{1-p},-\sqrt p),\quad e_N=(\sqrt p,\sqrt{1-p}),\quad m_P=m_N=1.
$$

It is optimal at budget $C(p)$ exactly when $c(p)\le0$, $p_z<p<p_s$, with value $g(p)$. To certify it, put $q=1-p$, $\eta=\eta_2(p)$, $c_H=c(p)$ and

$$
l_H=-\eta J_1(q)+2c_Hv'(p),\quad a_H=-\eta J(q)-l_Hq-2c_Hv(q),\quad b_H=a_H+l_H.
\tag{TB.6}
