One has $c_H>-vg\ge-\gamma/2>-\zeta$, and the two gaps have zero value and derivative at $q,p$. The value calculation uses $v'(p)(2p-1)-2v(p)=-1/(2v(p))$. Strict convexity proves both inequalities on the whole closed interval. Conversely the two tangencies force $\eta_2,c(p)$, so the test is necessary as well as sufficient.

For each $0<q<1/2$ there is a unique simple root $p=P(q)\in(p_z,p_s)$ of $T(p,q)=0$. Its formal masses and budget/value are

$$
\begin{gathered}
m=\frac{1-2q}{P-q},\quad\omega_\pm=\frac{2-m\pm m v(P)/v(q)}2,\\
e_P=(\sqrt{1-P},-\sqrt P),\quad e_{Q\pm}=(\sqrt{1-q},\pm\sqrt q),\\
L(q)=mJ(P)+(2-m)J(q),\quad Z(q)=mg(P),\quad\eta_3(q)=g(P)/B(P,q)=g_1(P)/\Delta_J(P,q).
\end{gathered}
\tag{TB.7}
$$

The masses of $e_P,e_{Q+},e_{Q-}$ are $m,\omega_+,\omega_-$ respectively. They form a measure exactly when $m\le1$; $m<1$ makes all three positive, since

$$
(2-m)^2q(1-q)-m^2P(1-P)=1-m,\qquad m>0,\quad2-m>0.
\tag{TB.8}
