At a feasible contact use $c_H=0$, $l_H=-\eta_3J_1(q)$, $a_H=-\eta_3J(q)-l_Hq$, $b_H=a_H+l_H$. The whole-interval gaps are

$$
\eta_3[J(t)-J(q)-J_1(q)(t-q)],\qquad
\eta_3[J(t)-J(q)-J_1(q)(t-q)]-g(t).
\tag{TB.9}
$$

They are strictly convex and tangent to zero at $q,P$. This proves global optimality for all measures, not merely finite trial families. The formal contact exists because $T(p_z,q)>0$, $T(p_s,q)<0$ and

$$
T_p=g''B-gJ''(p)<0,\qquad T_q=J''(q)[g-(p-q)g'].
$$

At a root $0<B<(p-q)\Delta_J$, so $T_q<0$ and $P'<0$. Differentiating $mP+(2-m)q=1$ gives

$$
Z'=g\left[-\frac{2-m}{P-q}+mP'\left(\frac{\Delta_J}{B}-\frac1{P-q}\right)\right]<0,\quad
L'=Z'/\eta_3<0,\quad\eta_3'=\eta_3(P-q)J''(q)/B>0.
\tag{TB.10}
$$

These analytic identities also hold on formally infeasible portions. They do not turn a negative weight into a measure. TC's polynomial-selected initial law and its slope/quadratic correction remain credited separately.

